// translations/tamil-approved.js — the canonical, static local Tamil
// translation layer for TOSP's curriculum content. This is the ONLY file
// where Tamil curriculum text lives — no view duplicates these strings.
//
// SOURCE-TRUTH RULE: data.js remains the sole authoritative English source.
// This file is a translation LAYER over it: every entry maps a stable
// contentId (see services/translation-service.js for how each is built) to
// its exact English source text and a faithful Tamil translation. Nothing
// here rewrites, paraphrases, shortens, or reinterprets the English
// business meaning — see docs/tamil-static-translation-map.md.
//
// Resolution: services/translation-service.js resolves a contentId to this
// file first; if no entry exists, English is shown with a concise
// "translation not available" message. There is no runtime network
// request, no provider, no approval gate — every entry here is
// immediately displayable.
//
// Object keyed by contentId (not an array) — at this scale (400+ entries)
// this avoids repeating contentId as both a key and a field, and every
// entry is O(1) to look up. See services/translation-service.js
// getStaticTranslation().
//
// Record shape (contentId is the object key, not a field):
// {
//   englishText: string,       // exact English source text this was translated from
//   tamilText: string,         // the Tamil translation
//   status: 'APPROVED_LOCAL',
//   sourceReference: string,   // citation, mirrors the data.js `source` field
// }
//
// Technical-term handling: company/product/platform names (Digitweb Lanka,
// Claude, Amazon, eBay, Shopify, B&Q, Seller Central), internal
// abbreviations (BGCT, ASIN, SKU, CTR, CVR, ACOS, TACOS, PPC, FBA, FBM),
// tool names (Helium 10, Terapeak, Google Trends, Jungle Scout), and named
// individuals (Mithusa) are preserved in their original form within Tamil
// sentences. Where a BGCT-specific term benefits from a Tamil gloss, the
// pattern used throughout is "தமிழ் விளக்கம் (English Term)" — e.g.
// "சிறந்த நடைமுறை (Best Practice)" — so the English term stays
// recognisable, per the CTR example in the requirements.

